\section{Introduction}

This document describes the unified processing pipeline applied to BOMNI~\cite{bomni2014} and PIROPO~\cite{piropo2014} datasets for evaluating projection configurations in fisheye pedestrian detection. Both datasets use rotated bounding box annotations provided by Tamura et al.~\cite{tamura2019omnidirectional}. Our processing follows a consistent two-step workflow with manual quality verification to ensure evaluation integrity.

\section{Common Processing Pipeline}

Both BOMNI and PIROPO undergo identical processing steps, ensuring methodological consistency for fair cross-dataset comparison.

\subsection{Step 1: Automated Preparation}

\subsubsection{Annotation Format Conversion}

\textbf{Input}: Tamura XML annotations in repurposed Pascal VOC format

\textbf{Process}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Parse XML files to extract rotated bounding box parameters
    \item Decode center and dimensions from xmin/ymin/xmax/ymax fields:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $center_x = (xmin + xmax) / 2$
            \item $center_y = (ymin + ymax) / 2$
            \item $width = xmax - xmin$
            \item $height = ymax - ymin$
        \end{itemize}
    \item Compute rotation angle: $\theta = \arctan2(dx, -dy)$ where $(dx, dy)$ is the vector from fisheye center to bbox center
    \item Convert to standard JSON format with explicit fields: \texttt{center\_x}, \texttt{center\_y}, \texttt{width}, \texttt{height}, \texttt{angle}, \texttt{class\_name}
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Output}: JSON annotation files with precomputed rotation angles

\subsubsection{Why Tamura's Format Requires Conversion}

\textbf{Tamura's format limitation}: The original Pascal VOC XML format does \textit{not} include an explicit rotation angle field. Tamura et al. repurposed the standard Pascal VOC bounding box fields (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) to encode the center position and dimensions of rotated bounding boxes, but the rotation angle itself is \textit{omitted}.

\textbf{Implicit angle encoding}: Tamura assumes all pedestrian bounding boxes follow the fisheye radial distortion pattern. The rotation angle can be reconstructed geometrically:
\begin{equation}
\theta = \arctantwo(dx, -dy) \quad \text{where} \quad
\begin{cases}
dx = \text{bbox\_center}_x - \text{fisheye\_center}_x \\
dy = \text{bbox\_center}_y - \text{fisheye\_center}_y
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

This $(dx, dy)$ vector points from the fisheye center to the pedestrian's center, and its angle determines the bbox rotation (height axis aligned radially).

\textbf{Why this design is problematic}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Computational redundancy}: The rotation angle must be recalculated via \texttt{arctan2()} \textit{every single time} annotations are loaded---during training (thousands of epochs $\times$ thousands of images), evaluation, and visualization. This represents massive redundant computation for a value that never changes.
    \item \textbf{Format ambiguity}: Standard Pascal VOC parsers will misinterpret the annotations as axis-aligned boxes, yielding incorrect results unless the user knows about Tamura's repurposing convention.
    \item \textbf{Lack of flexibility}: The implicit angle encoding enforces the radial constraint. If non-radial rotations are needed (e.g., for data augmentation or correcting annotation errors), the format cannot represent them.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Why Tamura chose this format}: Likely reasons include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Pascal VOC compatibility}: Adhering to an established annotation standard (Pascal VOC) for backwards compatibility with existing tools, despite the standard not supporting rotation.
    \item \textbf{Legacy tooling}: Reusing existing annotation tools that output Pascal VOC format rather than developing custom tooling.
    \item \textbf{Enforcing radial constraint}: Ensuring all annotators follow the fisheye radial pattern by making angle calculation automatic rather than manual.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Our conversion rationale}: We address these limitations by:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Precomputing angles once}: Calculate $\theta = \arctan2(dx, -dy)$ during the one-time conversion step and store it explicitly in JSON. This eliminates redundant trigonometric operations during training/evaluation (compute once, use thousands of times).
    \item \textbf{Explicit, self-documenting format}: Our JSON schema (\texttt{center\_x}, \texttt{center\_y}, \texttt{width}, \texttt{height}, \texttt{angle}, \texttt{class\_name}) is immediately clear and requires no special knowledge to interpret.
    \item \textbf{Unified cross-dataset format}: BOMNI and PIROPO annotations become identical in structure, enabling shared data loading code and consistent evaluation pipelines.
    \item \textbf{Performance optimization}: Faster data loading during training (critical for large-scale experiments with multiple projection configurations).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Computational savings}: For a dataset with $N$ images evaluated over $E$ epochs during training, our conversion eliminates $N \times E$ \texttt{arctan2()} calls. For PIROPO with 3,089 images evaluated 100 times (training + hyperparameter tuning), this saves $\sim$309,000 trigonometric operations.

\subsubsection{Frame Extraction and Filtering}

\textbf{Challenge}: Original datasets contain thousands of frames per sequence, but Tamura only annotated sparse keyframes ($\sim$10\% of total frames).

\textbf{Process}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Extract or organize frames from videos/downloads
    \item Identify annotated frames by matching filenames with Tamura annotation files
    \item \textbf{Delete all unannotated frames} (keeps only frames with verified ground truth)
    \item Organize frames to match annotation directory hierarchy
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Rationale}: Unannotated frames may contain unlabeled pedestrians (false ground truth negatives) or be truly empty. Including them without verified annotations would compromise evaluation integrity. We conservatively use only frames with explicit ground truth labels.

\subsubsection{Visualization Generation}

\textbf{Process}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Load each annotated frame and corresponding JSON annotations
    \item Draw rotated bounding boxes on images using OpenCV
    \item Save visualizations to dedicated output directory
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Output}: Visual representations of all annotations for human inspection

\subsection{Manual Quality Review}

\textbf{User task}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Inspect all visualization images
    \item Identify annotation errors:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Incorrect bbox position (misaligned with pedestrian)
            \item Wrong size (too large/small, loose vs. tight fit inconsistency)
            \item Incorrect rotation angle (not aligned with radial direction)
            \item False positives (background objects mislabeled)
            \item Missing detections (pedestrian visible but not annotated)
        \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Delete visualization images with incorrect annotations}
    \item Keep only visualizations representing correct ground truth
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Critical requirement}: User deletes \textit{only visualization images}, never annotation files directly. The automated cleanup step (Step 2) handles annotation removal.

\subsection{Step 2: Automated Cleanup}

\textbf{Process}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Scan remaining visualization images (after user deletion)
    \item Identify annotation files lacking corresponding visualizations (user deleted them)
    \item \textbf{Delete orphaned annotation JSON files and frame images}
    \item Regenerate final clean visualizations from verified annotations
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Output}: Production-ready dataset containing only manually verified correct annotations

\textbf{Rationale}: Manual verification filters annotation noise. Tamura annotations, like all manual labeling efforts, contain errors. Our BOMNI verification revealed a 25.5\% error rate. Evaluating on uncorrected annotations yields misleading metrics influenced by annotation noise rather than true model performance.

\section{BOMNI Dataset Processing}

\subsection{Dataset Overview}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Source}: Bogazici Omni-directional and Multi-Camera Network Images
    \item \textbf{Environment}: Single indoor room, ceiling-mounted fisheye camera
    \item \textbf{Resolution}: 640$\times$480 pixels
    \item \textbf{Fisheye center}: (320, 240)
    \item \textbf{Sequences}: scenario1 with 4 videos (top-0, top-1, top-2, top-3)
    \item \textbf{Tamura annotations}: 337 frames, 1,122 person instances (initial)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{BOMNI-Specific Steps}

\subsubsection{Frame Extraction from Videos}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Input: 4 MP4 video files (top-0.mp4 through top-3.mp4)
    \item Extract all frames using OpenCV (cv2.VideoCapture)
    \item Naming: 4-digit serial numbers (0001.jpg, 0002.jpg, ...)
    \item Output: Thousands of frames per sequence
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Cleanup and Conversion}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Delete unannotated frames (reduces from thousands to 337 annotated frames)
    \item Convert 337 Tamura XML files to standard JSON format
    \item Generate visualizations for all 337 frames
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Manual Verification Results}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Frames reviewed}: 337
    \item \textbf{Incorrect annotations identified}: 86
    \item \textbf{Error rate}: 25.5\%
    \item \textbf{Error types}: Wrong position, inaccurate size, incorrect orientation, false positives
    \item \textbf{Final verified dataset}: 245 frames, 834 person instances (BOMNI-production)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Sequence Aggregation}

\textbf{Strategy}: Combine all 4 sequences (top-0 through top-3) into unified BOMNI dataset

\textbf{Justification}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Per-sequence sample size insufficient ($\sim$60 frames each) for reliable AP/precision/recall
    \item Identical camera position across sequences (same fisheye center, distortion model)
    \item Aggregation captures pedestrian diversity (appearance, pose, scale variation)
    \item Research goal: evaluate projection generalization, not sequence-specific tuning
\end{itemize}

\subsection{BOMNI Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Limited scene variability}: Single room, constant lighting, uniform background
    \item \textbf{Low resolution}: 640$\times$480 (less detail than PIROPO's 800$\times$600)
    \item \textbf{Repetitive frames}: Near-duplicates from static pedestrians
    \item \textbf{Small dataset}: 245 images (modest compared to COCO's 118k training images)
    \item \textbf{Annotation noise}: 1--2\% residual error expected even after manual correction
\end{enumerate}

\section{PIROPO Dataset Processing}

\subsection{Dataset Overview}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Source}: People in Indoor ROoms with Perspective and Omnidirectional cameras
    \item \textbf{Rooms}: Room A (15$\times$10m), Room B (8$\times$5m)
    \item \textbf{Cameras}: 8 in Room A (3 omni + 5 perspective), 2 in Room B (1 omni + 1 perspective)
    \item \textbf{Resolution}: 800$\times$600 pixels
    \item \textbf{Fisheye center}: (400, 300)
    \item \textbf{Tamura annotations}: 3,089 frames, 3,550 person instances (omni cameras only)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{PIROPO-Specific Challenges}

\subsubsection{Massive Frame Archives}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Each camera folder: 2.8--4.9 GB (thousands of frames per sequence)
    \item Tamura annotated only sparse keyframes ($\sim$10\% of available frames)
    \item Unannotated frames drastically outnumber annotated ones
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Solution}: Extract \textit{only} frames matching Tamura annotation timestamps (identified by ISO 8601 filenames, e.g., \texttt{2015-05-12T12-17-03.626Z.jpg}). Reduces dataset from $\sim$15 GB to $\sim$1 GB.

\subsubsection{Hierarchical Folder Structure}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Original PIROPO: \texttt{Room A/omni\_3A/omni\_3A/omni3A\_training/}
    \item Tamura annotations: \texttt{Room\_A/omni\_3A/omni\_3A\_training/}
    \item Differences: Spaces vs. underscores, duplicate camera folders, sequence naming
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Solution}: Reorganize extracted frames to match Tamura's flattened structure with standardized naming (\texttt{Room\_A/\{camera\}/\{sequence\}/}).

\subsection{Camera Selection}

\subsubsection{Excluded: Perspective Cameras}

\textbf{Cameras excluded}: conv\_4A through conv\_8A (Room A), conv\_2B (Room B)

\textbf{Justification}: Our research objective is fisheye pedestrian detection. Perspective cameras exhibit minimal radial distortion and do not require gnomonic projection transformations. Including perspective data would dilute evaluation metrics with out-of-scope samples.

\subsubsection{Included: Omnidirectional Cameras Only}

\textbf{Cameras annotated by Tamura}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Room\_A: omni\_1A, omni\_2A, omni\_3A
    \item Room\_B: omni\_1B
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Total}: 4 fisheye cameras, 3,089 frames (pre-verification)

\subsection{Sequence Selection}

\subsubsection{Excluded: Unannotated Sequences}

\textbf{Sequences excluded}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item test4 through test12 (marked ``SEQ'' in PIROPO docs = sequences exist but no ground truth)
    \item training\_seats (sitting pedestrians, not annotated by Tamura)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Justification}: Quantitative evaluation requires ground truth. Sequences without Tamura annotations cannot contribute to precision, recall, AP, or IoU-based metrics.

\subsubsection{Excluded: Empty Background Sequences}

\textbf{Sequences excluded}: training\_illum, training\_bg, training\_additional\_bg

\textbf{Content}: Only background with illumination/appearance changes, no pedestrians

\textbf{Justification}: These sequences were provided for background modeling in classical motion-based detection (e.g., background subtraction). Our YOLO-based detector does not require background modeling. Frames without pedestrians contribute zero true positives and zero false negatives, providing no evaluation signal.

\subsubsection{Included: Annotated Pedestrian Sequences}

\textbf{Sequences annotated by Tamura}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{training}: Person walking exhaustively around room (3 people)
    \item \textbf{test2}: Up to 3 people simultaneously walking
    \item \textbf{test3}: 1 new person (not in training) walking
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Camera Aggregation}

\textbf{Multi-camera structure}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item 4 physical cameras at different ceiling positions
    \item Same sequences recorded \textit{simultaneously} from multiple viewpoints
    \item Same pedestrians captured from different angles and distances
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Strategy}: Combine all 3,089 annotations from all 4 cameras into unified PIROPO dataset

\textbf{Justification}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Statistical robustness}: Larger sample size (3,089 vs. $\sim$750 per camera) for reliable metric estimates
    \item \textbf{Viewpoint diversity}: Multiple angles stress-test projection configurations across varying radial distortion patterns
    \item \textbf{Research alignment}: Goal is optimal configuration for \textit{general} fisheye detection, not camera-specific tuning
    \item \textbf{Consistency with BOMNI}: BOMNI aggregates sequences; PIROPO aggregates cameras. Both maximize data for robust evaluation.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Secondary analysis} (optional): Per-camera performance breakdown as sanity check for camera-specific issues.

\subsection{Manual Verification (Pending)}

Following the same protocol as BOMNI:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Visualize all 3,089 frames
    \item Manual review and deletion of incorrect annotations
    \item Automated cleanup
    \item Expected error rate: 10--25\% (based on BOMNI experience)
\end{enumerate}

