\section{Methodological Consistency and Fair Evaluation}

\subsection{Why Consistency Matters}

Applying identical processing to BOMNI and PIROPO ensures:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Differences in projection configuration performance reflect \textit{true dataset characteristics} (scene complexity, pedestrian scale, lighting) rather than processing artifacts
    \item Cross-dataset validation of optimal configurations (if Config A wins on both datasets, stronger evidence of generalization)
    \item Fair comparison: Same annotation quality standard (manual verification), same evaluation protocol
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Conservative Data Inclusion}

Our approach prioritizes \textbf{evaluation integrity over dataset size}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Exclude unannotated frames (avoid false negatives from missing labels)
    \item Exclude incorrect annotations (avoid rewarding wrong predictions)
    \item Exclude irrelevant data (perspective cameras, empty backgrounds)
    \item Result: Smaller but \textit{cleaner} datasets with verified ground truth
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and Mitigation}

\textbf{Small dataset sizes}: BOMNI (245 images) and PIROPO ($\sim$3,000 images) are modest compared to COCO (118k) or ImageNet (1.2M).

\textbf{Mitigation strategies}:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Focus on \textit{relative} comparisons (projection configuration rankings) rather than absolute AP values
    \item Cross-dataset validation (consistency across BOMNI and PIROPO increases confidence)
    \item Conservative claims (acknowledge generalization limits beyond indoor fisheye scenarios)
    \item Statistical significance testing (if sample size permits)
\end{enumerate}

\section{Summary}

Both BOMNI and PIROPO datasets undergo rigorous two-step processing with manual quality verification:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Step 1}: Automated extraction, filtering, conversion, and visualization
    \item \textbf{Manual review}: Human inspection and deletion of incorrect annotations
    \item \textbf{Step 2}: Automated cleanup and final dataset generation
\end{enumerate}

Final production datasets contain only verified correct annotations:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{BOMNI-production}: 245 frames, 834 person instances
    \item \textbf{PIROPO} (post-verification): TBD frames ($\sim$2,300--2,700 expected)
\end{itemize}

This methodological rigor ensures evaluation metrics reflect true projection configuration performance rather than annotation noise or processing inconsistencies.

